/**

@page page_peano_domain_decomposition Domain decomposition

\tableofcontents

Peano uses one tree per MPI rank, a deterministic rank ownership map, and
dispatches over the partition of each rank. MPI is optional.

# Structural model

Every rank hosts one peano4::Spacetree whose tree identifier equals the rank.
The spacetree holds the refinement plan, and its ownership table assigns each
cell to one rank.

A rank materialises the levels above the spawn level in full, because that
spine carries the ownership decisions every rank needs. Below the spawn level
it materialises only its own blocks plus one ring of neighbours; a query about
any other region is answered from the refinement plan by lattice arithmetic.
The memory a rank holds therefore grows with its own block count, not with the
global grid.

The plan makes geometry, adjacency and communication order deterministic.
Every rank derives them from the same domain, refinement plan and rank count,
so the two ranks that hold one boundary face agree on the face sequence
without exchanging it.

@image html domain-decomposition/domain-decomposition.png

# Inter-rank partition

The spawn level bounds the replicated spine and the granularity of the
ownership plan. Rank ownership follows weighted cells in curve order. The
partition descends recursively through a cell when every child is refined; a
cell with any unrefined child remains indivisible, preserving
resolution-transition faces and coarsening families. Every rank derives the
same packed ownership table without communication.

Cells have one owning rank. A face that cells of two ranks touch exists in the
partition of both ranks with the local cell on one side; peano4::Partition
lists these faces per neighbour rank in canonical order (level, normal,
lattice), so neither the tree indices nor the slots have to match.

# Intra-rank parallelism

A phase runs tarch::accelerator::parallelFor and
tarch::accelerator::parallelReduce over the cell and face spans of the
partition; with the host backend these are OpenMP loops, with a GPU backend
device kernels. The partition build after a regrid runs one task per local
block through tarch::tasking.

# Boundary exchange

peano4::HaloExchange exchanges the halves of the faces two ranks hold of one
pool: `post()` gathers the half of the local side of every such face into one
send buffer per neighbour rank in the pool's memory location and starts the
non-blocking messages, `wait()` scatters the received halves into the other
side. The local cells of the partition run between the two calls, so the
messages travel while the rank computes. Cell data has no exchange because
each cell has one owner.

@image html domain-decomposition/replicated-boundary-data.png

# Regridding and migration

A changed refinement plan can also change the cell-weighted rank ownership.
peano4::Regrid names the cell slots that move to another rank in canonical
order, and peano4::MigrationExchange moves their data before the new faces
are rebuilt.

# Periodic domains

The two opposite cells on a periodic axis meet on one face with two adjacent
cells, as the tree aliases the periodic entities. A periodic pair inside one
rank therefore writes one face slot, and a pair that crosses rank ownership
uses the ordinary halo exchange.

# Rank-wide state

A dispatch reduces its per-cell results through `parallelReduce`; MPI-wide
values such as the admissible time step size use one collective per phase,
issued by every rank in the same phase. Grid and memory statistics apply the
same pattern after each phase.

# Analysis

The Grid Snapshot reports the cells, the faces, the levels, the MPI load
balance and the faces two ranks hold. @ref page_peano_runtime_analysis
explains these fields.

*/
